ANCHORS & RATIONALE
================================================================================

COST ESTIMATION APPROACHES:
  1. Reverse auction floor (market-revealed minimum)
  2. Current spending adjusted for efficiency gains
  3. International comparisons
  4. Bottom-up Advocate delivery model
  5. Metric validation cost study

DEMAND CURVE THEORY:
  - Advocates have structural incentive: capture healthspan gains
  - Reduces low-value, reactive care (ED, readmission, late diagnosis)
  - Increases prevention, early intervention
  - Supply curve unchanged; demand shifts left → lower equilibrium cost

MORAL HAZARD SAFEGUARDS:
  - Metrics inherently slow-moving (no lagging needed)
  - Advocates punished if region health deteriorates (re-bid, sanctions)
  - Researchers blinded to client; competitive bidding prevents collusion
  - Draconian penalties for data fraud
  - Patient freedom to seek external care (Advocate must earn retention)

COMPARATIVE BENCHMARKS:
  - Kaiser Permanente: 6–8 years longer life expectancy; 50–70% more Stage 1
    cancer diagnoses (via vertical integration)
  - Bundled payment models (BPCI, CJR): 56–60% cost/quality gains 
    (but episodic, not regional)
  - This model: Regional, long-term, full-spectrum incentive alignment

CONTINUITY MECHANISM:
  - Auto-transfer preserves care relationships
  - No opt-out: Simplifies transition; prevents adverse selection
  - Incoming Advocate's profit = keeping inherited population healthy
  - No lagging needed; continuous incentive works post-entry

